+
+  <!-- ================= BODY ================= -->
+  <div class="body">
+
+    <!-- ---------- COLUMN 1 ---------- -->
+    <div class="col">
+      <div class="card">
+        <div class="position">
+          <div class="lab">Our Position</div>
+          <p>Even accounting for rapid hardware progress, quantum computers will need a <em>quantum leap</em> &mdash; not just steady improvement &mdash; to meaningfully accelerate deep learning over the next <em>10&ndash;20 years</em>.</p>
+        </div>
+      </div>
+
+      <div class="card">
+        <h2>Why Ask Now?</h2>
+        <p>Deep learning's progress tracks <strong>compute</strong> &mdash; the &ldquo;bitter lesson.&rdquo; As classical hardware nears its limits, it is natural to ask whether <strong>quantum hardware</strong> could drive the next paradigm shift, as GPUs once did.</p>
+        <p>We present a <strong>first-of-its-kind survey</strong> mapping quantum algorithms onto every stage of the deep-learning pipeline, paired with <strong>quantitative economic-advantage forecasts</strong> built on real quantum hardware trends.</p>
+        <p>The verdict: today's promising speedups are blocked by overhead, missing memory, or apply only to narrow special cases.</p>
+      </div>
+
+      <div class="card tinted fig">
+        <img src="figs/fig1_qea.png" alt="Quantum Economic Advantage threshold">
+        <div class="figcap"><b>Fig 1 &middot; Quantum Economic Advantage (QEA).</b> Quantum gains are asymptotic but carry a huge constant hardware overhead. A problem pays off on quantum only beyond a critical threshold <b>N*</b> &mdash; below it, classical is faster.</div>
+      </div>
+
+      <div class="card accent">
+        <h2>The QEA Model</h2>
+        <p>We extend the Quantum Economic Advantage framework of <strong>Choi et&nbsp;al. (2023)</strong>. Quantum devices have asymptotic advantages but enormous constants:</p>
+        <ul>
+          <li><strong>~10<sup>13</sup>&times;</strong> slower gate speed vs. a floating-point op</li>
+          <li><strong>~10<sup>2</sup>&times;</strong> overhead from quantum error correction</li>
+          <li><strong>~10<sup>8</sup>&times;</strong> more GPU parallelism per dollar classically</li>
+        </ul>
+        <p>We forecast how <strong>qubit counts, gate speeds, and error rates</strong> evolve, then compute when&mdash;if ever&mdash;each algorithm crosses its threshold.</p>
+      </div>
+    </div>
+
+    <!-- ---------- COLUMN 2 ---------- -->
+    <div class="col">
+      <div class="card tinted fig f2">
+        <img src="figs/fig2_search.png" alt="Quantum vs classical search over time">
+        <div class="figcap"><b>Fig 2 &middot; Quantum vs. classical search.</b> Accounting for hardware trends and a 1-month runtime limit, Grover-type search reaches economic advantage only around <b>2034</b>, and only for problem sizes far larger than real deep-learning search.</div>
+      </div>
+
+      <div class="card">
+        <h2>Where Could Quantum Help?</h2>
+        <p>We assess the best available quantum option at each stage of the deep-learning pipeline &mdash; and its status today.</p>
+        <table>
+          <thead>
+            <tr><th>DL stage</th><th>Best quantum option</th><th>Speedup</th><th>Status</th></tr>
+          </thead>
+          <tbody>
+            <tr>
+              <td>Data processing</td>
+              <td>q-means; quantum PCA</td>
+              <td><span class="pill exp">Exponential</span></td>
+              <td>Blocked by QRAM</td>
+            </tr>
+            <tr>
+              <td>Hyperparameter &amp; action search</td>
+              <td>Grover; D&uuml;rr&ndash;H&oslash;yer</td>
+              <td><span class="pill quad">Quadratic</span></td>
+              <td>Infeasible (N&nbsp;&gt;&nbsp;10<sup>24</sup>)</td>
+            </tr>
+            <tr>
+              <td>General matmul / attention</td>
+              <td>Swap-test; Gao attention</td>
+              <td><span class="pill poly">Polynomial</span></td>
+              <td>Lost to overhead; QRAM limits</td>
+            </tr>
+            <tr>
+              <td>NN training (classical)</td>
+              <td>Allcock; Liu; Newton; Zlokapa</td>
+              <td><span class="pill mix">Mixed</span></td>
+              <td>Promising, but slow / restrictive</td>
+            </tr>
+            <tr>
+              <td>Quantum NNs / VQAs</td>
+              <td>VQAs; kernel methods</td>
+              <td><span class="pill unc">Unclear</span></td>
+              <td>No advantage in benchmarks</td>
+            </tr>
+          </tbody>
+        </table>
+        <div class="figcap"><b>Table 1.</b> Status of quantum acceleration across the deep-learning pipeline.</div>
+      </div>
+
+      <div class="card">
+        <h2>Robustness</h2>
+        <p>We vary every growth rate and constant in the model across <strong>10<sup>&minus;3</sup>&ndash;10<sup>4</sup></strong>. The predicted advantage year is <strong>largely insensitive</strong> to these changes.</p>
+        <p>The one real lever is the <strong>quantum gate-speed improvement rate</strong> (beyond Moore's law), which materially shifts the outlook for matrix multiplication. Grover predictions are robust &mdash; these algorithms are <em>time-bound, not qubit-bound</em>.</p>
+      </div>
+    </div>
+
+    <!-- ---------- COLUMN 3 ---------- -->
+    <div class="col">
+      <div class="card tinted fig">
+        <img src="figs/fig3_panels.png" alt="Advantage analysis by algorithm type">
+        <div class="figcap"><b>Fig 3 &middot; Advantage by algorithm class.</b> Problem sizes in the shaded region are feasible on a quantum computer; only those above the orange QEA line benefit. <b>(a)</b> exponential speedups can win by ~2033; <b>(b)</b> Grover wins only at enormous sizes (~2034); <b>(c)</b> dense matrix multiplication stays out of reach.</div>
+      </div>
+
+      <div class="card">
+        <h2><span class="num">3</span>Three Roadblocks</h2>
+        <div class="road">
+          <div class="rn">1</div>
+          <div class="rt"><b>Overhead swamps matmul.</b> Quantum matrix multiplication beats classical <em>O(N<sup>3</sup>)</em> asymptotically, but constant overhead makes it infeasible at any practical size for foreseeable hardware.</div>
+        </div>
+        <div class="road">
+          <div class="rn">2</div>
+          <div class="rt"><b>QRAM is missing.</b> The most powerful speedups &mdash; q-means, quantum PCA, HHL-based training, kernel methods &mdash; all depend on practical <em>QRAM</em>, which remains underdeveloped.</div>
+        </div>
+        <div class="road">
+          <div class="rn">3</div>
+          <div class="rt"><b>Special cases only.</b> Grover-type quadratic speedups need search sizes <em>&gt; 10<sup>24</sup></em> to pay off &mdash; far beyond real hyperparameter or RL action search.</div>
+        </div>
+      </div>
+
+      <div class="card accent">
+        <h2>Call to Action</h2>
+        <ul>
+          <li><strong>Accelerate QRAM</strong> &mdash; it gates most of the promising exponential speedups.</li>
+          <li><strong>Push quantum gate speed</strong> and cut error-correction overhead beyond current roadmaps.</li>
+          <li><strong>Target problems</strong> with both a clear asymptotic advantage <em>and</em> viable problem sizes.</li>
+          <li><strong>Stay skeptical</strong> of exponential &ldquo;training&rdquo; claims that rest on heavy preconditions.</li>
+        </ul>
+        <p style="margin-bottom:0;">Quantum's near-term role in deep learning is <strong>narrow</strong>. The opportunity is real &mdash; but realizing it requires a leap, not a step.</p>
+      </div>
+    </div>
+  </div>
